     1	VALIDATED
     2	
     3	# Proof report
     4	
     5	## What is proven
     6	
     7	For every finite `ValSeq` `VS` whose elements have K sort `Float`, and every
     8	K `Float` threshold `T`, the translated `has_close_elements(list(VS), T)`
     9	call reaches:
    10	
    11	```k
    12	hasClose(VS, T)
    13	```
    14	
    15	`hasClose` is the disjunction, over index pairs `i < j`, of the exact supplied
    16	float-semantics term:
    17	
    18	```k
    19	floatLt(absF(subF(VS[i], VS[j])), T)
    20	```
    21	
    22	Thus the result is true exactly when two distinct list positions have absolute
    23	difference strictly less than the threshold. This is a partial-correctness
    24	reachability theorem under the supplied MPY semantics.
    25	
    26	The target is `SPEC.target` in `spec.k`. Its initial configuration fixes the
    27	entry-point closure to the exact body translated from `solution.py`, the
    28	builtins binding, an empty heap and stack, `NoExc`, and exit code 0.
    29	
    30	## Program and translation identity
    31	
    32	- `solution.py` preserves the required
    33	  `has_close_elements(numbers: List[float], threshold: float) -> bool`
    34	  signature.
    35	- `solution.mpy` was regenerated by
    36	  `python3 py2mpy.py solution.py > solution.mpy`.
    37	- `HC-INNER-BODY`, `HC-OUTER-BODY`, and `HC-FUNCTION-BODY` in
    38	  `verification.k` are syntax aliases for the corresponding constructor
    39	  subtrees in `solution.mpy`; they do not rewrite an executing program term.
    40	- The input hashes still match `run-input.json`:
    41	  `prompt.py` is
    42	  `00b2e074e127a6a9d1376278bef732933760ab706057ec755a8c2642217b557a`,
    43	  and `py2mpy.py` is
    44	  `406485eac118431c08ef59f30f79dfa6aa9d5825408917fc5ce7c806e664db16`.
    45	
    46	## Proof-extension inventory
    47	
    48	| Extension | Class and domain | Justification and dependents |
    49	|---|---|---|
    50	| `allFloatVS` | Definitional summary, total on `ValSeq` | Structural domain predicate. Empty/cons equations are disjoint, exhaustive, and descending. Used by every claim and bridge guard. |
    51	| Guarded `applyBin("-", X:Val, Y:Val)` simplification | Derived lemma when both operands satisfy `isFloat` | It is the supplied `applyBin("-", F1:Float, F2:Float) => subF(F1,F2)` rule with sort membership exposed. Its RHS agrees with the supplied rule on the overlap. |
